# Linux - Streams 

## Questions: 

1. What is a stream in Linux?
2. What are the 3 data streams?
3. Create a new file new.txt and run the command ls missing_directory new.txt - this should give output to two different streams.
4. How can we direct each stream to a file (one at a time)?
5. How can we direct both 2 output streams to a file?
6. Which stream is directed to a file by default?
7. How can we direct both output streams to a file in one command?

## Answers:

1. A stream is a flow of data between a program and an input or output destination, such as a terminal or file. Linux uses streams to allow programs to receive input, display results and report errors. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html)
2. The three data streams are: 

        Stream  |Name               |File descriptor   |Purpose
        --------|-------------------|------------------|------------------------------------------
        `stdin` | Standard Input    | 0                | Receives input, usually from the keyboard
        `stdout`| Standard Output   | 1                | Displays normal command output
        `stderr`| Standout Error    | 2                | Displays error messages
    [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html)
3. `touch new.txt`, `ls missing_directory new.txt`. Error Message: `ls: cannot access 'missing_directory': No such file or directory
new.txt`.
    * new.txt is written into output.txt.
    * The error message still appears in the terminal.
[Reference Link](https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html)
4. Redirect Standard Output: `ls missing_directory new.txt > output.txt`. Redirect Standard Error: `ls missing_directory new.txt 2> errors.txt`.
   *  The error message is written into errors.txt.
   *  new.txt still appears in the terminal.
   *  The > symbol redirects output, and 2> specifically redirects standard error.
[Reference Link](https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html)
5. `ls missing_directory new.txt > output.txt 2> errors.txt`. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html)
6. Standard output (stdout, file descriptor **1**) is redirected by default when using **>** without a file descriptor number. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html)
7. `ls missing_directory new.txt > combined.txt 2>&1`
    * `>` redirects standard output to `combined.txt`.
    * `2>&1` redirects standard error (**2**) to the same destination as standard output (**1**).
[Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html)

